  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA 1030hPa high settles just off the coast today, giving us\nanother day of dry and pleasant fall weather with south-\nsouthwest flow supporting weak warm air advection. Even with\nsome increasing mid and high level clouds as moisture spills\nover the approaching ridge, temperatures will still likely warm\n10-15 degrees above normal into the low to mid 60s. Ridging\ncentered in the Ohio Valley amplifies and continues building \neastward tomorrow, sending a weak warm front into the Northeast.\nOverall moisture and forcing continue to appear rather weak with\nlatest NBM probabilistic guidance limiting chances for at least\n0.01\" of rain to the southern/western Adirondacks and Upper\nHudson Valley with chances ranging from 20-40% tomorrow\nafternoon. The front lifts north of eastern NY by tomorrow\nevening, with the ridge axis and warm sector shifting overhead \ninto Wednesday. There is increasing confidence that Wednesday\nwill be a beautiful day as large scale riding supports mainly\nsunny skies and the incoming 850hPa isotherms ranging 2 to 2.5 \nstandard deviations above normal per the NAEFS and ENS likely \nsupports well above normal temperatures. In fact, the latest \nNBM probabilistic guidance shows a 40 to 80% chance for highs to\nmeet or exceed 70 degrees for much of eastern NY and western \nNew England outside the higher terrain areas. \n\n\nTemperatures remain mild into Wednesday night with well above \nnormal temperatures likely continuing into Thursday as a cold \nfront slides eastward. The boundary is associated with a broad\nand weakening positively tilted trough over the Upper \nMidwest/Great Lake with the parent low occluding as the front \nmoves into the Northeast. Despite the weakening forcing and\nupper level dynamics, the moisture transport ahead of the front\nand thermal gradient along the boundary should be sufficient to\nsupport our first measurable rainfall event in three weeks! The\nfront looks to reach eastern NY by Thursday afternoon and heads\ninto western New England by Thursday evening as the weakened \nparent shortwave interacts and potentially phases with a \nnorthern stream shortwave in southern Canada. Latest ensembles \nand clusters still show a split solution regarding when the \nphasing occurs which will determine if the front slows down \nwhile it is still overhead or if it will have a chance to escape\ninto New England before slowing down. There is a large enough\nspread in the guidance to support widespread likely POPS Thurs \nnight with likely POPs focused along and east of the Hudson on \nFriday before trending lowering by Saturday. Latest guidance \nshows there is a 30 to 70% chance for at least a half inch of \nmuch needed rain between 7am Thursday and 7am Friday for much of\nthe area. Once the two shortwaves phase, there is good \nconsensus in a secondary low developing along the boundary \nresulting in more widespread rain; however, the latest trends \nsuggest this may occur when the boundary is far enough to our \neast to keep most of the impacts in New England. Even still, \nunsettled conditions look to continue over the weekend as the \nupper level parent low moves overhead supporting additional \nscattered showers and cool air advection that trend temperatures\nback towards normal.",
